name 'csaif_pl_custompy-L17-2_Python'
title 'title'
description 'description here'

markdown <<MARKDOWN
<h1>Calling Your Function in Main.py</h1>
<p>Now that you've defined a function with a parameter in <code style="font-size:14px;">custom.py</code>, let's look at how to actually call it from <code style="font-size:14px;">main.py</code>.</p>

<div style="max-width: 340px; margin: 20px auto; border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden;">
  <div style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-family: 'Courier New', Courier, monospace; font-size: 14px; padding: 8px 14px;">main.py</div>
  <div style="background-color:#1C1F2A; color:#FFFFFF; padding:14px 16px; font-family:'Courier New',Courier,monospace; font-size:16px; line-height:1.8;">
    <span style="color:#6FCAFF;">from</span> <span style="color:#F07FB0;">neighborhood</span> <span style="color:#6FCAFF;">import</span> <span style="color:#F07FB0;">Painter</span><br>
    <span style="color:#6FCAFF;">import</span> <span style="color:#F07FB0;">custom</span><br>
    <br>
    luna = <span style="color:#F07FB0;">Painter</span>()<br>
    luna.<span style="color:#F07FB0;">take&#95;paint</span>()<br>
    <br>
    <span style="color:#F07FB0;">custom</span>.<span style="color:#F07FB0;">move&#95;fast</span>(luna)<br>
    luna.<span style="color:#F07FB0;">paint</span>(<span style="color:#ACA8EA;">"blue"</span>)<br>
    <span style="color:#F07FB0;">custom</span>.<span style="color:#F07FB0;">move&#95;fast</span>(luna)<br>
    <span style="color:#F07FB0;">custom</span>.<span style="color:#F07FB0;">turn&#95;right</span>(luna)
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Step 1: Import the file</h2>
<p>In <code style="font-size:14px;">main.py</code>, you'll see that <code style="font-size:14px;">custom.py</code> is imported near the top of the file.</p>
<p>This allows you to use the functions that were created in the <code style="font-size:14px;">custom.py</code> file.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Call the function with the file name</h2>
<p>When you use a function from another file, the format is a little different.</p>
<p>You need to tell Python where the function is coming from by using the file name (<code style="font-size:14px;">custom</code>) followed by a dot (.), then the function name: <code style="font-size:14px;">custom.move&#95;fast(luna)</code></p>

<h2>Step 3: Pass in the object (like your painter)</h2>
<p>If the function needs a painter, you pass it in by putting the painter's name in the parentheses: <code style="font-size:14px;">custom.move&#95;fast(luna)</code></p>
<p>This tells the function which painter to use when it runs.</p>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;">
<p>➡️ This concept is covered in Lesson 7. You can find more details in the full <a href="https://studio.code.org/s/aif2-2025/lessons/7" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="font-weight:bold;">lesson plan</a>.</p>
</div>

<h2>Next up!</h2>
<p>Explore the backpack feature to see how to transfer your custom code to different levels.</p>
MARKDOWN
